      "Buck's first day on the Dyea beach was like a nightmare. Every hour was filled with shock and surprise. He had been suddenly jerked from the heart of civilization and flung into the heart of things primordial. These were not town dogs who took their ease in the shade; these were huskies, wolves in miniature, who knew no law but the law of club and fang.",
      "He had never seen dogs fight as these wolfish creatures fought along the freezing shore. There was no fair play; when a dog went down, fifty gray brutes swarmed over him, tearing his throat before he could rise. Curly, a sweet-tempered Newfoundland who had crossed the sea with Buck, made friendly advances toward a husky. The husky leaped in, slashed Curly's face open from eye to jaw, and leaped clear. In five seconds, thirty wolves had torn poor Curly to ribbons in the snow. Buck never forgot the sight: so that was the way—no fair play; once down, that was the end of you. Well, he would see to it that he never went down.",
      "Francois, the French-Canadian government courier, fastened Buck into the traces alongside Dave and Sol-leks. Spitz, a great white dog who had been brought from Spitzbergen, was the lead-dog. Spitz was treacherous and cruel, smiling while he planned murder in his heart. But Buck was quick to learn. Dave was an experienced wheeler who nipped Buck's legs whenever he made a blunder, while Francois wielded his whip with impartial justice.",
