      "The horror culminated on a wild, cold March night when Poole, Dr. Jekyll's terrified butler, arrived at Mr. Utterson's house. 'Mr. Utterson, there has been foul play!' cried the butler. 'For a week, someone has been locked inside my master's cabinet, weeping day and night, demanding strange chemical drugs from every chemist in London! The voice that answers through the door is not Dr. Jekyll's voice—it is the voice of Mr. Hyde!'",
      "Armed with an ax and a kitchen poker, Utterson and Poole broke down the heavy red baize door of the laboratory cabinet. Inside lay the twitching, contorted corpse of Edward Hyde, holding a shattered phial of cyanide in his claw-like hand! On the desk lay Dr. Jekyll's full confession: Jekyll had discovered a chemical tincture that could separate the two conflicting natures within man—the angel and the demon. Taking the drug, he had transformed his body into Edward Hyde, indulging in vile, unrestrained vices without risking his respectable reputation.",
      "But the evil persona had grown monstrously strong. The transformations began to occur spontaneously in his sleep, without the drug; and the supply of impure salt necessary to concoct the antidote was exhausted throughout England! Faced with the certainty of being permanently trapped inside the body of the murderous monster Hyde, Jekyll had penned his agonized confession and taken poison, leaving behind an immortal parable of the tragic duality of the human soul.",
