      "Mr. Wilson explained that he ran a small pawnbroker's shop in Coburg Square. Two months ago, his young assistant, Vincent Spaulding—a clever fellow who worked for half-wages so he could develop photographs in the dark cellar—had showed him an advertisement in the Morning Chronicle: The Red-Headed League offered a nominal salary of four pounds a week to any Londoner of sound body and mind possessing pure, blazing red hair, for purely nominal services.",
      "Wilson had applied, and out of thousands of red-headed candidates, was appointed by a Mr. Duncan Ross. His sole duty was to sit in an empty office in Fleet Street from ten till two every weekday, copying out the Encyclopedia Britannica in longhand! For eight weeks he copied out the letter 'A,' receiving his four sovereigns every Saturday. But this very morning, upon arriving at the office, he found the door locked and a cardboard placard pinned to it: 'THE RED-HEADED LEAGUE IS DISSOLVED. October 9, 1890.'",
